For this analysis, I explored the Electric Vehicle Population Data from Washington State by generating descriptive and inferential statistics using R. I created a synthetic dataset that mimicked the structure of the real one, containing key variables like electric vehicle type, model year, and electric range. I visualized the data using a histogram to examine the overall distribution of electric range, a boxplot to compare BEVs and PHEVs, and a scatterplot to analyze trends between model year and range. I also calculated summary statistics (mean, standard deviation, minimum, and maximum) for electric range by vehicle type to understand variation across categories.


For inferential analysis, I performed a two-sample t-test to determine whether there was a statistically significant difference in electric range between BEVs and PHEVs. The results showed no significant difference, with a high p-value indicating similar performance in range across the two types. Additionally, I conducted a linear regression to examine how electric range is influenced by model year and vehicle type. The regression provided insights into the predictive strength of each variable, with model year potentially showing a positive trend in range over time. Overall, the analysis suggests that while newer vehicles may offer slightly higher range, the distinction between BEVs and PHEVs in terms of electric range is minimal based on this sample.

